---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { ISSUES_URL } from "../releases";

const UPDATED = "October 17, 2026";

const promises = [
  { icon: "lock", title: "No data collected", body: "We don't receive your clipboard, your files or anything about how you use Quakboard." },
  { icon: "eye", title: "No tracking", body: "No analytics, no ads, no crash reports sent anywhere." },
  { icon: "device", title: "No account", body: "Nothing to sign up for. There's no server holding your data." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy · Quakboard"
  description="Quakboard's privacy policy: no data collected, no account, and sync only over your local network."
>
  <section class="shell pt-14 pb-12 text-center md:pt-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl" data-reveal>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Privacy policy</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-bold text-accent md:text-5xl">Your clipboard stays yours</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">
        This policy covers the Quakboard apps for macOS, Windows, Linux, iPhone
        and iPad, and this website.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-faint">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 text-left md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        promises.map((p, i) => (
          <article class="rounded-xl bg-surface p-6 shadow-sm" data-reveal data-delay={i * 60}>
            <span class="grid h-10 w-10 place-items-center rounded-full bg-accent text-accent-ink">
              <Icon name={p.icon} size={20} />
            </span>
            <h2 class="mt-4 font-semibold text-accent">{p.title}</h2>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-muted">{p.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="policy shell max-w-3xl pb-8">
    <h2>What's stored, and where</h2>
    <p>
      Quakboard keeps your clipboard history, settings and paired devices on
      your own device. Nothing is copied to us or to any cloud service.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>macOS:</strong> <code>~/Library/Application Support/com.dereckan.quakboard/</code></li>
      <li><strong>Windows:</strong> <code>%APPDATA%\com.dereckan.quakboard\</code></li>
      <li><strong>Linux:</strong> <code>~/.local/share/com.dereckan.quakboard/</code></li>
      <li><strong>iPhone and iPad:</strong> inside the app, removed when you delete it. Files you fetch are in the Files app under On My iPhone → Quakboard.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can delete any item from the app, and on a computer choose how much
      history is kept. Deleting the folder above removes everything.
    </p>

    <h2>Copying passwords and private text</h2>
    <p>
      Quakboard saves what you copy, which can include passwords and private
      messages. That history never leaves your devices except to the devices
      you pair. You can delete any item at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>Sync between your devices</h2>
    <p>
      Sync is off until you turn it on. Devices talk directly over your local
      network, never through the internet or a server. Only devices you pair
      with a code can send or receive, and everything is encrypted end to end.
      A device's name is shared only with the devices it pairs with.
      <a href="/sync">How sync works</a> has the details.
    </p>

    <h2>Clipboard access on iPhone and iPad</h2>
    <p>
      iOS doesn't let apps read the clipboard in the background, and Quakboard
      doesn't try to. It reads the clipboard only when you tap Paste, when you
      share to Quakboard from another app, or when Quakboard opens if you turned
      on "Send new copies when Quakboard opens". iOS asks for the Local Network
      permission so devices can find each other; it's used for nothing else.
    </p>

    <h2>When the apps use the internet</h2>
    <p>The iPhone and iPad app doesn't use the internet. The desktop app does, only for these:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Link previews.</strong> When you open a copied link in Quakboard,
        it loads that page to show its title, description and image. The
        website sees a normal visit from your IP address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Link icons.</strong> Next to each copied link, Quakboard shows
        the website's icon, loaded from that website. The website sees your IP
        address; no one else is involved.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Update checks.</strong> Quakboard asks GitHub whether a new
        version exists, and downloads it from GitHub if you choose to update.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lab features.</strong> If you turn on screen capture in Lab, its
        helper is downloaded once from GitHub.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of these requests include your clipboard history.</p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no cookies, no analytics and no tracking, and everything it
      shows is served from the site itself. The one exception: the Download
      page asks GitHub for the latest version number, so GitHub sees your IP
      address. The host that serves the site keeps standard server logs.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Quakboard doesn't collect personal information from anyone, including children.</p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If what the apps do with your data changes, this page changes with it,
      and the date at the top is updated.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy? Open an issue on
      <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub</a>, or see <a href="/support">Support</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .policy p,
  .policy ul {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .policy ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .policy li {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .policy strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .policy a {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .policy code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
